<h2>How to Audit Your Own GBP Categories</h2>

<p>You do not need a data science team to figure out whether your current categories are working for you. Here is a simple, repeatable framework you can run for your own market.</p>

<h3>A Note on the Evidence Behind This Guide</h3>

<p>What follows synthesizes published local-SEO research — primarily Whitespark's ranking factors survey and Sterling Sky's category testing — with general best-practice patterns commonly observed in how well-structured, well-ranking home service profiles are set up. We call out real, named sources wherever a specific finding is attributed to them. Where we offer a recommendation without a named source, treat it as directional guidance rather than a guaranteed outcome: Google's algorithm changes constantly, and your specific market and competitors will always have the final word.</p>

<h3>Steps to Run Your Own Category Audit</h3>

<ol>
<li><strong>Scrape competitor data:</strong> Use a tool like Local Falcon, BrightLocal, or the GBP API to collect the top 20 Map Pack results for your 5 target keywords.</li>
<li><strong>Document categories:</strong> Use the GMB Everywhere browser extension to view the exact primary and secondary categories your competitors are using.</li>
<li><strong>Score specificity:</strong> Rate each primary category from 1 (broad, like "Contractor") to 5 (specific, like "Emergency Plumbing Service").</li>
<li><strong>Track positions over time:</strong> Record your Map Pack positions weekly for 8-12 weeks after any category changes you make.</li>
<li><strong>Control for variables:</strong> Hold your review requests, posting schedule, and website updates constant during the test period so you can actually tell what the category change did.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Key Principles: What Actually Impacts GBP Category Rankings</h2>

<p>Here are the principles that matter most for home service contractors, drawn from published local-SEO research and consistent patterns observed across well-optimized profiles.</p>

<h3>Principle #1: Your Primary Category Is Your Highest-Impact Controllable Signal</h3>

<p>The logic here is straightforward, and it is confirmed repeatedly by local SEO practitioners and by Whitespark's survey data: your primary category tells Google what kind of business you are before it evaluates anything else. A plumbing business that lists itself as "Contractor" is asking Google to guess what it actually does. A business that lists itself as "Plumber" removes the guesswork entirely.</p>

<p>In practice, the more specific and accurate category consistently outperforms the generic one, and the effect tends to compound when that specific category exactly matches what people are typing into Google. If an even more specific category is genuinely accurate for your business — something like "Tankless Water Heater Repair Service" instead of "Plumber" — and it reflects a meaningful share of your revenue, it is worth testing as your primary.</p>

<h3>Principle #2: Category Dilution Is Largely a Myth for Relevant Categories</h3>

<p>Sterling Sky's testing found that adding relevant secondary categories did not hurt existing rankings — if anything, new categories brought additional visibility into related searches. The real risk is adding categories that are loosely related or clearly irrelevant to your business.</p>

<p>The safest rule of thumb: every category you add, primary or secondary, should be something a real customer would genuinely search for when looking for your services. If you would not put it on your storefront sign, it probably does not belong on your profile.</p>

<h3>Principle #3: More Secondary Categories Isn't Automatically Better</h3>

<p>Google allows up to 9 secondary categories, but filling all 9 slots is not an advantage in itself. A common pattern in well-optimized profiles is somewhere in the range of 3-6 categories that are all genuinely and specifically relevant to services the business actually offers.</p>

<p>Padding your profile out to 7-9 categories by including marginally related ones tends to blur your profile's focus rather than sharpen it. Treat your category count as a quality filter, not a quota to fill.</p>

<h3>Principle #4: Changing Your Primary Category Causes Temporary Volatility</h3>

<p>By general local-SEO consensus and consistent anecdotal reporting from practitioners, changing your primary category tends to cause a period of ranking volatility. Rankings for your old primary keyword may dip before rankings for your new primary keyword catch up, and it can take a couple of weeks for things to stabilize.</p>

<p>Only change your primary category if you are moving to something more specific and more accurate. Switching to a broader, less-specific category is the change most likely to backfire, and it is worth avoiding entirely during your busiest season.</p>

<h3>Principle #5: Attribute Completion Is a Meaningful, if Secondary, Signal</h3>

<p>Profiles with more complete, accurate attribute data tend to perform better, largely because attributes function as filters rather than a direct ranking multiplier. When someone searches "plumber who offers free estimates," Google can only surface businesses that have that attribute enabled — everyone else is excluded from that specific result set regardless of how strong their reviews or proximity are.</p>

<p>Service-based attributes — free estimates, online appointments, onsite service — map most directly to how people actually search, so they are worth prioritizing over more cosmetic attributes.</p>

<h3>Principle #6: Seasonal Category Switching Can Work for Weather-Driven Trades</h3>

<p>For trades with a genuine seasonal split in demand, some HVAC contractors switch their primary category with the season — "Air Conditioning Repair Service" in summer, "Heating Contractor" in winter — on the logic that matching category specificity to seasonal search intent sharpens relevance exactly when it matters most.</p>

<p>This is a more aggressive, higher-maintenance tactic, and it works best in markets with real seasonal swings, like the upper Midwest or Northeast. In mild climates, or for trades without strong seasonality, the disruption from frequent category changes is probably not worth the benefit.</p>

<h3>Principle #7: Your Reviews Should Reinforce Your Categories</h3>

<p>Your category tells Google what you do. The language in your reviews acts as a secondary confirmation signal. A roofing contractor whose reviews consistently mention "roof repair" reinforces the relevance of a "Roof Repair" category, while a contractor with generic five-star reviews that never mention a specific service gives Google less to work with.</p>

<p>This is part of why, when you ask happy customers for a review, it is worth prompting them to mention the specific service they received rather than leaving it fully open-ended.</p>

<h2>Category Recommendations by Trade: Plumbers, HVAC, Electricians, and Roofers</h2>

<p>Generic advice fails because every trade has different category options, seasonal patterns, and customer search behavior. Here is what we recommend for four of the most common home service trades.</p>

<h3>Plumbers: Choosing Primary and Secondary Categories</h3>

<p>The most commonly used primary categories among plumbing businesses are "Plumber," "Plumbing Service," and "Emergency Plumbing Service." For most plumbers, "Plumber" is the safer default — it is the category most likely to match how the average customer actually searches.</p>

<p>"Emergency Plumbing Service" is worth using as your primary only if emergency, after-hours work makes up the bulk of your revenue. It sharpens your relevance for urgent searches like "emergency plumber," but it can cost you visibility for generic "plumber near me" searches, since it signals a narrower specialty.</p>

<p><strong>Primary category recommendation:</strong> Use "Plumber" unless emergency work represents the majority of your revenue. If you are genuinely 24/7 emergency-focused, "Emergency Plumbing Service" is worth the tradeoff for generic visibility.</p>

<p><strong>Secondary category stack worth considering:</strong></p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Drain Cleaning Service</strong> - a high-frequency search category for most plumbing businesses</li>
<li><strong>Water Heater Installation/Repair</strong> - typically strong search volume relative to competition</li>
<li><strong>Pipe Repair Service</strong> - captures burst pipe and leak searches</li>
<li><strong>Sewer Service</strong> - relevant if you handle sewer line work</li>
<li><strong>Septic System Service</strong> - only add if you genuinely offer septic work</li>
<li><strong>Emergency Plumbing Service</strong> - add as secondary if it is not your primary</li>
</ol>

<h3>HVAC Contractors: Seasonal Strategy</h3>

<p>HVAC has the most complex category dynamics of any trade in this guide, because a single "HVAC Contractor" primary category has to serve very different seasonal searches — "air conditioning repair" in summer, "furnace repair" in winter — equally well, and it often cannot do that perfectly.</p>

<p>Some contractors address this by switching their primary category with the season, on the theory that matching category specificity to seasonal demand improves relevance exactly when it matters. This is an aggressive, higher-maintenance tactic. A simpler and still solid default is to keep "HVAC Contractor" as your primary category year-round and rely on secondary categories and predefined services to cover both sides of the season.</p>

