<h2>Attribute Analysis: Which GBP Attributes Are Worth Your Time</h2>

<p>Attributes are among the most underused signals in Google Business Profiles. While categories tell Google what you do, attributes tell Google how you do it and who you serve. Filling them out completely and accurately is one of the lowest-effort, highest-leverage things you can do to your profile.</p>

<h3>How Attributes Influence Rankings</h3>

<p>Google uses attributes in three main ways. First, as filter criteria: when a user searches for "wheelchair accessible plumber," only profiles with the "Wheelchair-accessible entrance" attribute appear in that filtered result set. Second, as relevance signals: a profile with "Free estimates," "Online appointments," and "Onsite services" sends a stronger service-intent signal than a bare-bones profile. Third, as fuel for AI-generated answers: Google's AI Overviews for local queries pull directly from attribute data.</p>

<p>A complete attribute profile does not guarantee a higher position in every head-to-head ranking comparison, but it directly determines whether you are even eligible to appear in filtered and AI-generated results that reference those attributes — which is a meaningful form of visibility impact in its own right.</p>

<h3>High-Impact Attributes by Category</h3>

<p>Not all attributes carry the same weight. Here is a rough grouping by how directly each type maps to common search intent.</p>

<div class="cost-table">
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Attribute Type</th><th>Examples</th><th>Why It Matters</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Service Options</td><td>Online appointments, Onsite services, Free estimates</td><td>Maps directly to common search modifiers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Offerings</td><td>24/7 service, Emergency service, Warranty</td><td>Matches urgent, high-intent search phrases</td></tr>
<tr><td>Planning</td><td>Accepting new customers, Appointment required</td><td>Sets expectations and signals availability</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accessibility</td><td>Wheelchair-accessible entrance, Parking</td><td>Required to appear in accessibility-filtered searches</td></tr>
