</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p>Service options and offerings tend to matter most because they map directly to how people phrase urgent, high-intent searches. If someone searches "plumber free estimate," the "Free estimates" attribute is a literal match.</p>

<h3>Identity Attributes</h3>

<p>Identity-based attributes like "Women-led," "Veteran-owned," and "Black-owned" are surfaced in dedicated Google search filters. Google has said it built out this kind of filtering in response to real growth in search interest for identity-specific businesses. Whether or not an identity attribute moves your ranking position directly, it can improve your visibility to people specifically looking for a business like yours.</p>

<p><strong>Important:</strong> Only add identity attributes that are true and verifiable. Google has suspended profiles for false identity claims.</p>

<h3>The Predefined Services Feature</h3>

<p>In 2025, Google rolled out predefined services for more categories. Instead of manually typing "drain cleaning," plumbers can now select "Drain Cleaning" from a dropdown that Google recognizes as a structured entity. This matters because predefined services generally match search queries more reliably than custom text, since Google can map them directly to entities it already understands.</p>

<p><strong>Action step:</strong> Log into your GBP dashboard, go to the Services section, and select every predefined service that applies to your business. Then add custom services for anything not covered by predefined options.</p>

<h3>Attributes and AI Overviews</h3>

<p>As Google's AI-generated overviews become a bigger part of local search results, attributes matter even more. Google's AI pulls structured data from GBP to generate answers like "Here are three plumbers in Atlanta who offer free estimates and 24/7 emergency service." If your profile lacks the attributes an answer is matching on, you are not eligible to be mentioned, no matter how strong your reviews or rankings are otherwise.</p>

